e had their treatment delayed by  the pandemic is this an admission that  non-covered patients have been let down  well i think this is about one of the  impacts that we were just talking about  sophie when you asked  about the other impacts of restrictions  and this is what happened earlier  on you know we know we've got a backlog  of people who have missed  whether it's mental health treatments or  elective surgery or scans  so what we're doing is investing 3  billion pounds into the nhs  next year to help them make a start on  tackling that  this will provide enough funding to do a  million  uh treatment scans surgeries to make a  start on tackling that backlog  a lot of that is also for for mental  health treatments that have been missed  um and it's right that we do that but as  it as you rightly point out  what this illustrates is one of the  other costs of what we've had to do to  tackle coronavirus alongside the  economic costs  you'll also of course be laying out that  an economic cost  and talking about the state of the  public finances which we both know is  going to be a pretty  sobering read three quarters of a  million people have lost their jobs  during the pandemic  and national debt is more than two  trillion pounds that's bigger than the  entire uk economy of gdp borrowing is at  its highest  level since records began  how have we ended up with what seems to  be the worst of all worlds a wrecked  economy  and the highest clover death toll in  europe  well i think look when it comes to the  economic impact  in common with many other countries  we're seeing a significant impact this  year  now you know one thing that is  particular about our economy  is social activities you know for  example the ones we've been talking  about going out having a meal seeing  people  they comprise a slightly larger share of  our economy  than they do for other countries  obviously that's the part of our economy  that's had the most impact of all of  this so it's unsurprising that we're  seeing  uh the results that we are you know the  initial lockdown we had  in duration was longer than other  countries that will of course  uh have had an impact as well and i  think slightly more  technically um but still i think a point  of worthy of node is the way in which we  calculate  impacts on gdp is slightly different to  other countries and i  need to probably go into it in detail  here but there's a way we calculate  activity in the public sector is  different which means that some of our  numbers  may actually look a lot worse when you  compare them to other countries but on a  genuinely  comparable basis they wouldn't quite be  a starker difference but i said look  no one is pretending that has not been a  significant impact on our economy  there has that's why we have stepped in  to provide the support to protect  millions of jobs and people's  livelihoods  and business as best we can through this  period so what are you going to  do uh from wednesday onwards to try and  rebalance  and deal with the public the finances  are you looking at tax cuts are you  looking at sorry tax risers should i say  spending cuts or both well  next week is about spending review which  is it's more focused on providing  spending budgets for departments next  year and because of the uncertainty  that we're experiencing at the moment we  took the decision to make that just a  one-year spending review  so most departments will see their  budgets rolled over and that's because  our primary focus at the moment is  rightly on responding to coronavirus so  providing the funds we need for the  health response whether that's  test and trace mass testing vaccines the  nhs  or indeed protecting people's jobs  businesses and livelihoods  as we've talked about that's the  priority for next week but you will also  see us  getting on with delivering on what we  were elected to do which is invest in  public services  and invest in infrastructure around the  country to level up spread opportunity  and help drive our economic recovery  one of your biggest challenges as  chancellor if not the biggest challenge  is going to have to  chart a way forward for the economy  after this  huge economic shock that has happened  and there's been lots of reports about a  proposed and planned  public sector pay freeze frances o'grady  was on just before you talking about why  she thinks that would be  a really bad idea and of course many of  these workers that she was talking about  people like  teachers refuse collectors prison  officers these are people who've been  working  on the front line during lockdown  you and the prime minister have both  said you don't want to return to  austerity but for many of these people  the hallmark of austerity  was seeing a pay freeze so if it looks  like austerity if it sounds like  austerity  if it feels like austerity then it is  austerity isn't it  well i i don't think that's fair  characterization at all and first of all  you know  i've worked with francis closely uh  during this crisis on various things so  very much  you know listen to what she has to say  and respectfully disagree in some  in some areas i think you will not see  austerity next week what you will see  is an increase in the government  spending on day-to-day public services  you know quite a significant one coming  on the increase that we had last year so  i think there's absolutely no way in  which anyone could say  uh that's austerity we're spending more  money on public services  uh than we were and then you know with  regard i can't comment on  future pay policy in advance of the  spending review but what i would say  is when we when we launched the spending  review i did say to departments that  when we think about public safe  settlements i think it would be entirely  reasonable to think about those in the  context of the wider economic climate  i think that's a reasonable thing to do  and then also secondly  i think it would be fair and francis i  think use the word fairness as well i  think it would it would be fair to also  think about what's happening with wages  with jobs with hours  across the economy when we think about  what the right thing to do  in the public sector is so listening to  the end part of that answer  are you considering a pay freeze for  public sector workers  as i said so if you i can't comment in  advance of pay policy being set at the  spending review and i'll just point you  to this you're not ruling it out  when we when we launch i mean you can  ask me any question and say are you  ruling out ruling it in  i mean those are the the points i made  you know we were talking  you are also talking about fairness and  you said that you're looking at wages  and you're looking at what would be the  right thing to do there  as i said yes when we launched a  spending review we said when we think  about public sector pay  with that should be done in the context  of the overall economic climate i think  that's an entirely reasonable  uh thing to do and also as francis also  talked about  fairness as as we discussed you know we  need to see what's going on with wages  with jobs with employment with hours  across the economy when we calibrate and  figure out what the best thing to do for  public sector is as well  your manifesto at the last election said  we will not raise the rate of income tax  vat  or national insurance so are you going  to stick to that promise and if you are  does that mean that you're going to have  to at some point look at other taxes  such as  potentially capital gains tax  so if i you know as you whenever i'm on  here and you try and ask me these things  i'm sorry i have to give you the same  answer i can't comment on  future fiscal policy uh for all the  obvious  reasons and i think it's probably right  for people to remember the next week is  a spending review it's not  uh it's not a budget and in the spending  what we're doing is setting out the  department's budgets  for next year and as i said what you'll  see is more investment in day-to-day  public services  getting on with our manifesto investing  in infrastructure to spread opportunity  around the country drive our recovery  but primarily the focus will be on  fighting coronavirus  so funding for our health response and  also protection for people's jobs and  their livelihoods  through this crisis that's our focus and  it should be our focus at this time  um i've got a feeling that i might get a  similar answer to this next question but  i am going to want to ask it anyway  because i think it's so important to  so many people across the country the  government increased  universal credit by 20 pounds a week  because of  the coronavirus pandemic but that's due  to end  next april according to the joseph  roundary foundation that could push  700 000 people including 300 000  children into poverty are you looking at  extending that  well what i'd say is you're absolutely  right and that was one of the steps that  we took at the beginning of this crisis  it was one of a number of things we did  to provide support to  people you know through through the  acute phase of this crisis  it runs all the way to april as you as  you said and  you know so there's no need to make a  decision on it now many of the things  that we've put in place whether  it's furlough or the others they run  through the winter into the spring  and we keep everything under review and  we'll make the decisions that are right  for the economy  i think one thing we know and have  learnt this virus is  adapting evolving our ability to combat  it is adapting and evolving  so there's an enormous amount of  uncertainty at the moment and it  wouldn't be right to make long-term  decisions  right now but of course as we get  through and we'll have  future events like budgets where we can  take stockholm and see what's the right  way  both to support the economy but  importantly to support people  in their jobs in employment and support  businesses as we try and hopefully  emerge from this and get back to growing  again there's also been a lot of  speculation about  what's going to happen with foreign aid  it's going to read what your manifesto  said  we will proudly maintain our commitment  to spend 0.7  of gross national income on development  are you going to stick to that promise  again you know  we've got a spending review on on  wednesday where we'll talk about all the  budgets  i'm asking about your manifesto in an  election that was just last year  yeah and and you're right to read that  out and you and i am proud of our  commitment and i think we've seen that  in the in the last few months and we've  played i think a leading role  in helping to combat this uh pandemic  not just at home but also abroad as well  whether it's helping to provide  vaccines to other countries using the  brilliance of our researchers here to  get treatments to places around the  world  so lots of different ways that we've  demonstrated leadership and made a  difference  uh to some of the world's poorest  countries at what has been a very  difficult time and  that will never you know that will that  will never stop whilst we're  whilst we're in government okay um  just finally if the minister breaks the  ministerial code  should they remain a minister well  you'll know that the prime minister is  the ultimate arbiter of the ministerial  code that's how it's set out  and the prime minister is the one that  will make that judgment  on a case-by-case basis and i think  obviously you'll be  asking i'm sure about the home secretary  the prime minister's reviewed  the findings of the independent advisor  has come to the  conclusion that he has and the home  secretary has rightly offered her a full  and unreserved apology  and and the promise said that that as  far as he's concerned means that the  matter should be  you know put a line under and we should  move on and i'm glad to hear that the  culture has improved in the home office  and we're getting back to  doing what people want us to do which is  focus on keeping their streets safe  fighting crime um good to say that  things have improved at the home office  after people found out what was  happening there before and you say the  prime minister is the ultimate arbiter  of the ministerial code  but for many people that's kind of the  issue that they're worried about  and because you can see why some might  question whether he's approached this  from a neutral perspective  in march when the inquiry was launched  so well before  it found anything out he said that  pretty patel was doing an  outstanding job and i have every  confidence in her i am sticking by her  that's before the conclusions  according to whitehall sources when the  report was published he tried to water  it down and then  he asked conservative mps to form a  square around the pritster  is there one rule for boris johnson or  is there one rule  for friends of boris johnson and another  rule for everyone else  well look there was a review conducted  by an independent  advisor that and the findings of that  review have been published  and as the system works it's right that  the prime minister  as the person who is in charge and runs  a government  is able to receive that report go  through the findings and make a decision  that he thinks is right weighing up all  the considerations  you know that that's what he's done the  home secretary has offered a full and  unreserved apology  for what's happened and i think at that  point you know we we can move on  and it's a focus on the things that  people want us to do which is keeping  their streets safe and that's what the  home secretary is doing that's what the  government  is doing i can understand why and the  government might want to move on for  from this but it does feel that there  may be still questions to answer on it  the report from the government's now  former advisor on standard said  my advice is that the home secretary has  not consistently met the high standards  required by the ministerial code  her approach on occasions has amounted  to behavior that can be described  as bullying so on what basis has the  prime minister reached a different  conclusion  well i think the prime minister's  reviewed everything and has come  to the conclusion that he has and he  doesn't believe that a different  conclusion  is and respectfully it is a different  conclusion he doesn't believe the home  secretary  is a bully but the home secretary has  offered a full and unreserved  uh apology for what's happened and  i think at that point you know the  finest has made his decision and we  should get back to focusing on on what  people  on people what people want okay uh rishi  cena we are  out of time but thank you very much for  coming on the program ahead of such a  busy week  for you thank you
 

